<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-11-20T07:32:33Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Uganda minister at COP31: 'We need a just transition'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:61ea5742d486842c2bc8e6e0b5958b70c6d0260fcf7d1cd898e2da3061753f9d</id><updated>2026-11-20T07:32:33Z</updated><published>2026-11-19T19:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/uganda-minister-at-cop31-we-need-a-just-transition/a-79997209--312e033bb073.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/uganda-minister-at-cop31-we-need-a-just-transition/a-79997209?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Funding from wealthy countries to help deal with the impacts of climate change hasn't materialized, says Uganda's environment minister. The East African country is hoping oil and carbon credits will help boost finances.</summary><category term="Climate" /></entry><entry><title>Axes and cutlasses: Violence on Nigeria's election campaign trail</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:846a07c8c2fc45b515943ae6a4e7485f18e4946a9ba0ac842c7cd867790e2ff7</id><updated>2026-11-20T07:31:29Z</updated><published>2026-11-18T10:56:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/axes-and-cutlasses-violence-on-nigeria-s-election-campaign-trail/a-79978467--351fdc554734.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/axes-and-cutlasses-violence-on-nigeria-s-election-campaign-trail/a-79978467?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As Nigeria's January 2027 elections approach, political violence and thuggery at campaign rallies and town hall meetings raise concerns about voter safety.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria's upcoming election marred by rising AI disinformation</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:229044eb65f29fe020e51c060c2c09d06a6ce00955bf9d10d2d64b446e7d10e6</id><updated>2026-11-20T07:31:29Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T21:05:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nigeria-s-upcoming-election-marred-by-rising-ai-disinformation/a-79946897--e1618bd315dd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-s-upcoming-election-marred-by-rising-ai-disinformation/a-79946897?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As Nigeria inches closer to the 2027 polls, the use of artificial intelligence in the electoral process has prompted questions about integrity and institutional readiness. Can Nigerians trust their democratic processes?</summary><category term="World" /></entry></feed>
